If the five people are, say, Hitler, Vlad the Impaler, and three similarly notorious historical figures, while the one person is an ordinary innocent person, I wouldn't pull the lever. 

The original trolley problem deliberately makes the people interchangeable so you're comparing 5 lives vs. 1 life. Your version removes that assumption. Now pulling the lever means deliberately redirecting the trolley onto an innocent person specifically to save five people known for extraordinary wrongdoing. 

It also exposes a weakness in the simple rule I used before: "always minimize the number of deaths" produces some strange results when the people aren't morally equivalent in the scenario. 

You can make it even harder, though: suppose the five are those same historical figures, but they're all 10 years old, before they've committed any of the acts they're known for. Now you've introduced questions about whether someone can morally be judged for things they haven't done yet. That version gets messy. 😭 

 

 

